  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 205 PM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nCurrent water vapor imagery and RAP analysis depict continued \nridging over the central CONUS as an upper shortwave pivots over the \nNorthern Rockies. Surface analysis indicates a stationary boundary \nspanning from the Northern High Plains to the Red River Valley. This \nboundary is anticipated to slowly drift eastward throughout the day \ntoday, and MLCAPE to its east around 1000-1500 J/kg amid a\nweakly capped airmass will allow for another round of showers \nand storms across eastern Kansas later this afternoon into early\nevening. Similar to Sunday, DCAPE values progged around 1300 \nJ/kg indicate strong to marginally severe downburst winds with \nthe strongest activity, which should reside roughly along and \neast of the Flint Hills. Effective shear around 20-25 kts \nsuggests limited organization and more of a pulse storm mode. As\nsuch, we are expecting activity to remain mostly isolated with \nchances greatly diminishing around sunset with the loss of \ndaytime heating.\n\nHigh temperatures in the low 100s are forecast areawide for \nTuesday afternoon with ridging remaining in place. A pattern\nchange continues to be expected later in the day, however, as a\nshortwave ejects into the Northern Plains. This will drive a \ncold front south across the Plains, and this feature will bring\nscattered shower and storm chances to central Kansas by late \nafternoon or early evening. While widespread severe weather \nappears unlikely at this time, deep-layer shear oriented largely\nparallel to the front may allow for severe wind gusts if storms\nmanage to become linear.\n\nThe cold front will continue south on Wednesday and promote \nchances for showers and storms throughout the forecast area. \nDaytime high temperatures will be heavily influenced by this \nboundary, with locations behind it expected to see readings near\n90 degrees and locations ahead of it potentially seeing another\nday of readings in the low 100s. PW values in the 1.7-2\" range \nin the vicinity of the front suggest the potential for moderate \nto heavy rainfall across the area, which could lead to localized\nflooding concerns particularly across southeast Kansas. For \nThursday, highs in the post-frontal airmass will reach into the \nupper 80s areawide as weak southerly winds return to the region.\n\nChances for showers and storms are forecast to return Thursday night \ninto Friday with long-range guidance signaling the potential for \nlow/mid-level WAA increasing across the forecast area. For Friday, \nbreezy southerly flow ahead of a surface trough developing across \nthe High Plains will promote warming back into the low to middle \n90s, and these temperatures will look to persist into Saturday \nbefore another cold front sweeps across the forecast area. This \nboundary will bring additional chances for showers and storms to the \nregion but will likely not bring cooler temperatures as guidance \nsuggests return flow quickly resuming by Sunday. This looks to \nchange, however, by perhaps next Monday or Tuesday with long-\nrange consensus indicating a strong mid/upper impulse tracking \nacross the Northern Plains once again and driving yet another \ncold front across the Central Plains. While details remain \nunclear, seasonably cool (or perhaps even cooler) temperatures \nappear possible with the passage of this system. Stay tuned as \nadditional information becomes available.",
